---
import { LlmPatternDemo } from "../../components/LlmPatternDemo";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="What is an LLM - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="what-is-an-llm">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        LLM: Large Language Model. It is the technology behind tools like ChatGPT,
        Claude, and Gemini. But what is it actually?
      </p>

      <p>
        An LLM is a program that predicts the next word one at a time, based on
        the previous words. That's it. (Strictly speaking it works in{" "}
        <strong class="text-stone-800">tokens</strong>: chunks of text that are
        often word fragments rather than whole words. Thinking in terms of words
        is close enough to build the right intuition, and we'll come back to
        tokens when we talk about context windows.) It was trained by reading enormous
        amounts of text: books, websites, code, conversations, and from all of
        that, it learned patterns. When you ask it a question, it doesn't
        "think" or "look up the answer." It generates a response one word at a
        time, each word chosen based on what's most likely to come next given
        everything before it.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is a deceptively simple idea that produces surprisingly
        sophisticated results. The same mechanism that lets it finish your
        sentence also lets it write code, summarize documents, and explain
        concepts. It's all pattern completion — just at a scale that makes it
        feel like understanding.
      </p>

      <h3>
        The Biggest Misunderstanding
      </h3>

      <p>
        Most people assume that when you ask an AI a question, it searches
        through some kind of database or knowledge store and retrieves the
        answer. Like a search engine, but smarter.
      </p>

      <p>
        An LLM doesn't store facts in a database. It doesn't have a filing
        cabinet of "things it knows." During training, it absorbed billions of
        text patterns and compressed them into numerical weights: billions of
        numbers that encode statistical relationships between words and
        concepts. When you ask "What is the capital of France?", it doesn't look
        up France in a table. It generates "Paris" because in the training data,
        the pattern "capital of France" was overwhelmingly followed by "Paris."
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why AI can be confidently wrong. It doesn't know what it knows.
        It just generates what fits the pattern. If the pattern is strong
        (capital of France), it's almost always right. If the pattern is weak or
        ambiguous (a specific contract clause, a niche API detail), it might
        generate something that looks right but isn't.
      </p>

      <h3>
        How It Processes Your Request
      </h3>


      <p>
        When your question reaches the model, it doesn't follow a simple lookup
        path. Modern LLMs use an architecture where your input passes through
        many layers of processing. At each layer, the model is essentially
        asking: "Given everything I've seen so far in this text, what patterns
        are most relevant?" It weighs different parts of your input against each
        other, figures out what relates to what, and builds up an increasingly
        refined understanding of what response would best complete the pattern.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't need to understand the math. The practical takeaway is this:
        the model processes everything you give it at once, weighing every word
        against every other word. This is why being clear and specific in your
        prompts matters so much: you're not just giving instructions, you're
        shaping the pattern the model uses to generate its response.
      </p>

      <LlmPatternDemo client:visible />

      <h3>
        How We Got Here
      </h3>

      <p>
        Language models aren't new. Researchers have been working on programs
        that process and generate text for decades. Autocomplete on your phone,
        spam filters, basic chatbots; these all used earlier forms of language
        modeling. But for a long time, they were very limited. They could handle
        short phrases but fell apart with anything complex.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things changed everything. First, in 2017, researchers at Google
        published a paper called{" "}
        <em>"Attention Is All You Need"</em> that introduced the <strong
          class="text-stone-800">transformer architecture</strong
        >: a new way for models to process text that could weigh the importance
        of every word relative to every other word, no matter how far apart they
        were. This solved a fundamental problem with earlier approaches and
        became the foundation for every major AI model since.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second, researchers discovered that these transformer models got
        dramatically better as you made them bigger and trained them on more
        data. Not just slightly better; qualitatively different. A small model
        might spit out grammatically correct nonsense. A large enough model
        could explain quantum physics, write working code, and carry on nuanced
        conversations. This phenomenon, called <strong class="text-stone-800"
          >scaling</strong
        >, is why the "Large" in Large Language Model matters.
      </p>

      <h3>
        The GPT-3 Moment
      </h3>

      <p>
        In June 2020, OpenAI released GPT-3. It had 175 billion parameters (the
        numerical weights we mentioned) and was trained on a huge portion of the
        internet. For the first time, a language model could do things that
        genuinely surprised people. You could ask it to write an essay, and it
        would write a coherent essay. You could give it a few examples of a task
        and it would figure out the pattern without any additional training.
      </p>

      <p>
        But GPT-3 was mostly an API for developers. The real inflection point
        came in November 2022, when OpenAI released <strong
          class="text-stone-800">ChatGPT</strong
        >, a chat interface on top of a refined version of the same technology. It
        reached 100 million users in two months, making it the fastest-growing consumer
        application in history at the time. Suddenly everyone, not just developers,
        could interact with a language model directly.
      </p>

      <h3>
        Where Things Stand Now
      </h3>

      <p>
        Since ChatGPT's launch, the field has moved at an extraordinary pace.
        Anthropic released Claude, Google released Gemini, Meta open-sourced
        Llama, and dozens of smaller companies have entered the space. Models
        have gotten faster, cheaper, more capable, and better at following
        instructions. The current generation can use tools, browse the web,
        write and run code, and work through complex multi-step problems.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the core technology is still the same: transformer-based pattern
        prediction, trained on large amounts of text, generating responses one
        token at a time. Everything else: the chat interfaces, the tool use, the
        "thinking" is built on top of that foundation. Understanding that
        foundation is what makes you effective at using these tools.
      </p>

      <h3>
        What About "Thinking" Models?
      </h3>

      <p>
        You'll notice some models pause and produce a stream of reasoning before
        they answer, labelled something like "thinking" or "reasoning." This
        isn't a different kind of intelligence bolted on. The model is still
        predicting one token at a time; it's just generating a scratchpad of
        intermediate steps for itself first, then using that as context for the
        final answer. Working through a problem step by step gives it more
        relevant patterns to build on, which is why reasoning models tend to do
        better on multi-step tasks like debugging or math.
      </p>

      <p>
        The practical takeaway: thinking mode trades speed and cost for a better
        shot at hard problems. Turn it on when the task has real steps to work
        through, and skip it when you just need a quick, direct answer.
      </p>

      <h3>
        A Peek Inside: the "J-Space"
      </h3>

      <p>
        For a long time these models were almost entirely black boxes. We could
        see what went in and what came out, but not what happened in between.
        That's starting to change. In July 2026 Anthropic published research
        showing that Claude appears to keep a small, privileged internal
        workspace, which they call the{" "}
        <strong class="text-stone-800">J-space</strong>, that holds the handful
        of concepts it's actively reasoning about, while the rest of its
        processing (grammar, fluent phrasing, fact recall) runs automatically
        underneath.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is early research, not a settled fact, and you don't need it to use
        these tools well. But it's a useful reminder that there's real structure
        behind the pattern-prediction, and it points toward a future where we
        can audit what a model is actually working on, catching things like a
        model noticing it's being tested or reacting to a prompt injection that
        never shows up in its visible output. If you're curious, Anthropic's
        write-up is worth a read:{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/global-workspace"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700"
          >A global workspace in language models</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h3>
        Common Myths
      </h3>

      <p>
        Working with AI tools becomes much easier once you let go of some common
        assumptions. Here are the ones that cause the most trouble:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "It understands what I'm saying"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            It doesn't understand things in the same way you do. It recognizes
            and reproduces patterns in language. The result often looks like
            understanding, and for practical purposes, you can sometimes treat
            it that way. But there's no comprehension behind the curtain. This
            is why it can write a beautiful paragraph about a topic and then get
            a basic fact about that topic wrong in the next sentence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "It remembers our previous conversations"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Every conversation starts from zero. When you open a new chat, the
            model has no memory of anything you've discussed before. Some tools
            (like ChatGPT and Claude) save your conversation history and re-send
            previous messages to simulate continuity, but that's the application
            doing the work, not the model. The model itself is stateless every
            single time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "It searches the internet for answers"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            By default, no. An LLM generates responses purely from its training
            data patterns. Some tools add web search as an extra feature
            (ChatGPT's browsing, for example), but that's a bolt-on capability,
            not inherent to the model. When a model without web search tells you
            something, it's generating it from patterns, not looking it up. Two
            consequences follow. First, every model has a training cutoff date
            and is effectively blind to anything that happened after it. Second,
            even for information from before that date, the knowledge isn't
            necessarily in there. Training doesn't guarantee a given fact was
            absorbed, so it's safer to treat the model's knowledge as possibly
            present rather than reliably present. This is why web search and
            handing the model your own source material matter so much.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "If it says something confidently, it's probably right"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Confidence and accuracy are completely unrelated in an LLM. The
            model generates the most probable next words, and "probable" often
            means "sounds authoritative." It will state a completely fabricated
            statistic with the same tone it uses for well-established facts.
            This is called hallucination, and we cover it in depth in a later
            chapter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "Longer prompts get better results"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            More words don't mean better output. What matters is clarity and
            relevance. A focused three-sentence prompt often outperforms a
            rambling paragraph. The model weighs every word you give it. Noise dilutes the signal. We'll cover this properly in the prompt
            engineering chapter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div
            class="text-sm font-semibold text-red-400 mb-1 line-through decoration-red-300"
          >
            "The same prompt always gives the same answer"
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Usually it won't. The model picks each token from a range of likely
            options with an element of randomness, so the same prompt can
            produce a different response every time you run it. Most of the time
            the answers are equivalent, but it also means a one-off good (or bad)
            result isn't guaranteed to repeat. Don't read too much into a single
            run.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
